# Question: over three years, is it cheaper to pay for a SaaS tool or to build
# and maintain our own, and how likely is building to be the cheaper option?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario product/buy-vs-build
# Change an assumption, for example a smaller team using the tool:
#   ubc --set 'seats = 50 to 100' --scenario product/buy-vs-build
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens build --scenario product/buy-vs-build
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros over the
# three years. Only the money is compared: building also takes the team away
# from the product for the months it lasts, which is not priced here.
#
# Sources: the SaaS vendor's public price list for the licence; loaded cost is
# salary plus 25-40% overheads (rule of thumb); the other ranges are typical
# experience.

# Assumptions
years = 3                         # horizon of the comparison; a common planning choice
seats = 100 to 250                # people who need the tool; count them in your company directory
licence = 30 to 60                # SaaS price per seat a month; check the vendor's public price list
integration = 10k to 30k          # connecting the SaaS to our systems and single sign-on; typical experience
build_cost = 120k to 300k         # engineers × months × loaded cost (salary plus 25-40%, rule of thumb)
maintenance = 15% to 30%          # yearly share of the build cost for fixes, upgrades, security; rule of thumb

# Calculation
buy = integration + seats * licence * 12 * years               # licences for every seat, every month
build = build_cost * (1 + maintenance * years)                 # building it and keeping it alive

# Result
buy
build
P(build < buy)                    # how likely is building to be the cheaper option?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   buy = ≈ 260k   90% [155k … 442k]   98% [120k … 550k]   ▃▅▇███▇▅▄▃▃▂▂▂▁▁
#   build = ≈ 312k   90% [192k … 509k]   98% [150k … 620k]   ▃▄▆▇██▆▆▅▃▃▂▂▂▁▁
#   P(build < buy) = 0.33
